# HTTPS for the devbox control plane. Two modes — pick ONE issuer block below:
#
# (A) PUBLIC box, BARE IP — no domain needed. Let's Encrypt issues
#     publicly-trusted certificates for IP addresses (GA since 2026-01) via the
#     "shortlived" ACME profile: ~6-day lifetime, auto-renewed by Caddy (needs
#     Caddy >= 2.10). ACME validation (http-01 / tls-alpn-01) always happens on
#     ports 80/443, so both must be reachable from the internet even though the
#     site itself serves on :4000.
#
# (B) PRIVATE box (VPN / Tailscale only, 80/443 closed) — DNS-01 with a DOMAIN.
#     Caddy proves ownership by writing a TXT record at the DNS provider, so
#     only OUTBOUND traffic is needed. Requires: the domain's DNS at a provider
#     Caddy has a plugin for (Cloudflare shown), a Caddy build with that plugin
#     (`caddy add-package github.com/caddy-dns/cloudflare` swaps /usr/bin/caddy
#     in place; then `apt-mark hold caddy` so a package upgrade can't replace it
#     with a plugin-less binary), and a DNS-edit API token scoped to that one
#     zone, handed to Caddy via a root-only env file + systemd drop-in:
#       /etc/caddy/cloudflare.env (0600):      CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=…
#       /etc/systemd/system/caddy.service.d/cloudflare.conf:
#         [Service]
#         EnvironmentFile=/etc/caddy/cloudflare.env
#     Normal 90-day certs (Caddy renews a month early). IP certificates CANNOT
#     be issued over DNS-01: use the domain everywhere (site addresses below,
#     gen-env-sites.sh's host arg, DEVBOX_PUBLIC_HOST, and the MCP URL clients
#     use). Keep the domain's A record "DNS only" (grey cloud) — a proxied
#     record points at Cloudflare, which can't reach a private box and doesn't
#     forward :4000 / :9xxx anyway. Note `caddy validate` runs without the
#     systemd env and reports the token as '' — the service itself is fine.
#
# Pair this with devbox-server bound to loopback so raw HTTP is unreachable
# from outside — in the server's env (e.g. /etc/devbox/devbox.env):
#
#   DEVBOX_BIND=127.0.0.1
#   DEVBOX_PORT=4001
#
# Then clients keep using the familiar port, now with TLS:
#
#   claude mcp add --transport http katalyst https://<your-ip>:4000/mcp \
#     --header "Authorization: Bearer $DEVBOX_API_TOKEN"
#
# This secures the CONTROL PLANE (API token, MCP, session streams, UI).
#
# To ALSO serve the per-env WordPress sites over HTTPS (TLS phase 2): a
# certificate is valid for any port, so Caddy can terminate TLS for the whole
# env port range and proxy port-for-port to loopback. Three steps:
#
#   1. gen-env-sites.sh <your-ip> <host[,host...]> <lo>-<hi> > /etc/caddy/env-sites.caddy
#      (match the server's WP_PORT_RANGE; hosts = the bare IP, and/or a domain
#      pointed at it), and uncomment the import below.
#   2. In the server env: DEVBOX_PUBLIC_SCHEME=https — new envs then bind
#      their published ports to 127.0.0.1 (Caddy owns the public side) and
#      get https wpUrl / admin-login links.
#   3. Rebuild any warm pools (they bake the scaffold at build time).
#
# Envs created BEFORE the switch keep publishing their ports on 0.0.0.0 with
# http URLs (their record says so) — pick a range slice for the proxy that
# doesn't overlap ports docker already binds publicly, or migrate those envs.
#
# With a DOMAIN (A records for @ and * → this box), list it alongside the IP
# in the site addresses below and in gen-env-sites.sh's <host,...> argument —
# both keep working, and DEVBOX_PUBLIC_HOST=<domain> puts it in returned URLs.

{
	email you@example.com
	# Issuer is GLOBAL on purpose, not per-site tls blocks: per-site tls
	# directives make Caddy reject the config when the same hostname spans site
	# blocks with different host sets ("appears in more than one automation
	# policy"), and gen-env-sites.sh's block relies on inheriting this.

	# (A) public box, bare IP: Let's Encrypt "shortlived" profile (~6-day
	#     certs; the bare-IP cert REQUIRES it; domain certs are short-lived too).
	cert_issuer acme {
		profile shortlived
	}

	# (B) private box, domain only — replace the block above with:
	# acme_dns cloudflare {env.CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN}
}

# import /etc/caddy/env-sites.caddy

# Control plane. Mode (A) with a domain, list both:  <your-ip>:4000, <your-domain>:4000 {
# Mode (B): the domain only:                           <your-domain>:4000 {
<your-ip>:4000 {
	# JSON payloads (session transcripts, env lists) compress ~10x; Caddy
	# skips compression for SSE streams automatically.
	encode zstd gzip
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:4001
}
